<TopicPage slug="subjects/sociology/advanced/globalisation-and-society">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Globalisation is <strong>the stretching of social relations across the planet</strong> —
    economy, culture, migration, media — and sociology studies what it does to identity,
    inequality and belonging. The honest finding: it's older than assumed, less uniform than
    advertised, and its backlash is as much its product as its trade flows.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="waves, compression, glocalisation, backlash" widget="equationStep" config={globCfg} />

  <h2>What sociologists track in the current wave</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Migration and diaspora:</strong> ~281 million international migrants; diasporas sustain dual identities, remittance economies and transnational families — the "here AND there" life is now statistically normal for hundreds of millions.</li>
    <li><strong>Global cities vs everywhere else:</strong> Sassen: London, New York, Singapore concentrate command functions — globalisation made certain cities richer and many regions relatively thinner.</li>
    <li><strong>Culture flows in all directions:</strong> K-pop, Nollywood, Bollywood — not one-way Westernisation; the "reversal of flows" is measurable in what the world watches.</li>
    <li><strong>The environmental connection:</strong> planetary problems (the environmental-science ladder) are globalisation\'s physical shadow — sociology of climate traces the same interdependence in carbon as in capital.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Harvey, The Condition of Postmodernity (1989); Robertson (1995); Sassen, The Global City (1991); IOM World Migration Report.</p>
</TopicPage>
